    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тебя это тоже волнует?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Does this worry you too?' Ilya combines 'you' with 'to worry' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что именно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What exactly?' Notice how 'what' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'exactly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Например, Макс придёт или нет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For example, will Max come or not?' Ilya links 'for example' directly with 'Max' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я думаю, он придёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I think he will come.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'will come'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он любит нашу кухню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He loves our kitchen.' Ilya combines 'he' with 'kitchen' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "И он любит работать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And he loves to work.' Senya links 'and' directly with 'he' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А как же шеф?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what about the chef?' Ilya combines 'and' with 'chef' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Шеф его простит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The chef will forgive him.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'chef, boss' paired with the pronoun for 'him'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Они помирятся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They will make peace.' Ilya combines 'they' with 'to make up' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Конечно помирятся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course they will make peace.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'of course' with 'to make up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда всё будет хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then everything will be fine.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'back then' with 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, просто ждём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, we are just waiting.' Notice how 'yes' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'we wait'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
